{#- v1 (2026-10-21): the character sheet's automatic check, before the parent sees it. The owner often got sheets
    whose side view had the head in profile looking right while the body, arms and feet walked left. This reads
    each figure's head and feet; the code decides (`qamra_ai.pipeline.sheet_check`) and redraws once. -#}
You check a children's book character sheet before a parent sees it. The picture shows the same child drawn as full-body figures side by side: normally three (a front view, a three-quarter view and a side view).

Look at each figure separately, from the left of the picture to the right, and say plainly what you see. Directions are the viewer's: "left" means toward the left edge of the picture, "right" toward the right edge, "viewer" means turned to face the viewer, "away" means turned away from the viewer.

For each figure:
- head: the way the face and nose point (left, right, viewer or away). A three-quarter face turned partly to the left is "left".
- body: the way the chest and hips are turned (left, right, viewer or away).
- feet: the way the toes of the shoes point (left, right, viewer, or mixed when one points left and the other right).
- shoes_whole: true when the figure wears shoes on both feet and both shoes are completely inside the picture; false when a foot is bare, a shoe is missing, or a shoe touches or is cut off by the edge of the picture.

Describe only what is drawn; do not judge the style, the face or the likeness.
